1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.200
The Winawer pins the knight, then tears open the queenside.

2
00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:08.400
This is the French Winawer Pawn Break.

3
00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:12.600
The first moves look normal. Develop, challenge the center, and wait.

4
00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:16.800
Natural moves hide the real idea.

5
00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:21.000
The pieces point at the target. One loose defender is enough.

6
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:25.200
The bait appears. It looks free, but it is the trap's waiting move.

7
00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:29.400
The reply is forcing. Calculate the next check.

8
00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:33.600
The point is simple: the position is built around space, timing, and activity.

9
00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:37.800
The king has fewer choices. Every escape square is covered.

10
00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:42.000
And now the tactical idea lands.

11
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.200
The tempting move fails because the position was built for this tactic.

12
00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:50.400
Save the pattern. Spotting it one move earlier is the point.

13
00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:54.600
Would you see it before taking the bait?
